            <name>
                <baseName xml:lang="en">Radioactive waste, in final repository for nuclear waste SF, HLW, and ILW</baseName>
            </name>
            <classificationInformation>
                <common:classification>
                    <common:class level="0" classId="115026cd-ce3b-3f7c-b95d-677f836961c8">nuclear waste</common:class>
                    <common:class level="1" classId="25c09900-3573-3b97-82a4-66b522f05375">unspecified</common:class>
                </common:classification>
            </classificationInformation>
            <common:generalComment xml:lang="en">For operation is meant the input of the waste and the refilling of the tunnels. The waste volume includes always the steel or concrete container. The density of SF is approximately 7.6 tonne per m3, of HLW is about 7.1 tonne per m3, and of ILW is 2.5 tonne per m3. Considering the amounts of the three different waste class streams for the Swiss 192 GWa scenario, the average density for the radwaste to be disposed of in this geological repository is calculated as about 5.4 tonne per m3. The total electricity supplied from the grid has been estimated on the basis of the necessary power and the duration of the operation. For the transport of HLW and ILW, an average distance of 70 km between the intermediate repository and the preliminarly identified site has been considered. Seepage water and methane to air are assumed to be negligible.;
Synonyms: Conditioned Spent Fuel, High level radioactive waste, Intermediate level radioactive waste; 
UUID: 62835b0e-1eaf-40a3-b89b-0dec99d2cc14</common:generalComment>
        </dataSetInformation>
